(Title) Tj
T*
(Augmented Reality Enhanced by Mirror Visual Feedback for Upper Limb Recovery Post-Stroke:) Tj
T*
(A Multigroup Randomised Rehabilitation Trial) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Forty-four stroke survivors with moderate-to-severe arm and hand weakness were randomised) Tj
T*
(to receive one of three rehabilitation approaches over six weeks: mirror visual feedback) Tj
T*
(combined with interactive augmented reality exercises, augmented reality alone, or) Tj
T*
(conventional physical therapy. Each intervention included 18 sessions of 90-minute) Tj
T*
(duration performed three times weekly. Objective motor recovery was measured using) Tj
T*
(standardised clinical scales assessing upper limb movement quality, functional strength,) Tj
T*
(and postural stability. Sensory testing evaluated proprioception and tactile perception.) Tj
T*
(Additionally, participants completed self-report questionnaires regarding functional arm) Tj
T*
(use in daily activities and quality-of-life perceptions. The combined mirror-plus-) Tj
T*
(augmented-reality group demonstrated significantly greater improvements in motor control) Tj
T*
(and balance capacity compared to conventional treatment. The augmented-reality-only group) Tj
T*
(also exceeded conventional therapy on these measures. Within interactive technology) Tj
T*
(groups, the mirror-enhanced approach showed superior outcomes for fine motor dexterity and) Tj
T*
(touch sensation. No therapy-related injuries or adverse events occurred. Improvements were) Tj
T*
(sustained at three-month follow-up assessment in all groups. These results suggest that) Tj
T*
(combining mirror visual principles with interactive digital environments produces) Tj
T*
(synergistic rehabilitation benefit for stroke survivors with moderate motor impairment.) Tj
